  bcf-naming-generalization: the shipped K-forest amplitude family is
    spelled with BCF names at every layer - BCFSpec, BCFForestSpec,
    BCFState, BCFForestCombiner, BCFSpecStorage, createBCFSampler,
    ChainStateData::hasBCF and the state format's "bcf" block - so each
    reads "BCF" where it means "carries amplitudes". The debt and its
    mitigation (every capability probe is totalAmplitudes() != 0, never
    a name or a forest count) are described in
    docs/design/multiplier-combiner.md. BCFSpecStorage
    (R_interface_bartcore.cpp:2128; used at :2165, :2233, :2262, :2953,
    :3095) is the per-build backing store whose vectors a BCFSpec's
    pointers borrow - each forest's column subset, interaction pairs,
    block partition and transposed basis - one more BCF-prefixed name
    for the same general capability. The R side carries the identical
    tell: isBCFSampler (R/bartcore.R:24) and refuseBCFMutation
    (R/bartcore.R:34), the latter's five call sites R/bartcore.R:289,
    :310, :387 and R/dbarts.R:1067, :1522, every one of which reads or
    gates on data@bases rather than a forest count or a name. A rename
    is ENGINE work, not a docs edit: a --preclean, a structSize move on
    the state fixtures
    (common.cpp already tripped once, 272 -> 344), and a state-block key
    change needing the same in-place re-encode M4.3 used. ONE LEG IS
    USER-VISIBLE, added 2026-08-14 off M4.4's residue audit:
    calibrationMapName (R_interface_bartcore.cpp) returns the literal
    "two-forest calibration map" for every non-softmax coupling, and it
    reaches a user through setForestPriorScale's refusal, which tells a
    K = 5 sampler its scale comes from the two-forest map "which owns
    both halves of its calibration". Wrong for K != 2 since the general
    family shipped, not falsified by M4.4, so it was deliberately NOT
    folded into that slice. A string plus whatever test pins it.
    Pre-release, unscheduled.
